name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 7 - a RE-QUESTION on a live anchor.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` at or below 0.50 AND sense
# at least +0.50 above it. FAR side = the baseline cleared the question itself. NEAR side =
# the baseline was held down but our arm did not reach either.
#
#   cycle 1  mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak    base 0.8125  sense 1.000  d +0.1875  FAR
#            ask named the call SHAPE ("a scope, a finder, an aggregate, a constant on the
#            class"); baseline transcribed it into one anchored regex, 13 of 16 in call 8.
#   cycle 2  mastodon.yaml.line-not-file.bak       base 0.158   sense 0.158  d  0.000   NEAR
#            ask refused to name a shape; baseline ran TEN different guessing greps and got
#            3 of 19. The baseline half of the window was CLEARED. It failed on the
#            instrument: step 1 asked for "the lookup surface it exposes", the sense arm
#            answered literally, walked sense_graph(callers) on five finder methods, and
#            NEVER called sense_blast on the class. Only cycle on record inside the window
#            on the baseline side.
#   cycle 3  mastodon.yaml.table-altitude.bak      base 0.091   sense 0.091  d  0.000   NEAR
#            description said "stored" and "rows in"; BOTH arms audited the accounts TABLE
#            (schema, foreign keys, db/views, arel, find_by_sql) and answered a different
#            question in agreement.
#   cycle 4  mastodon.yaml.names-the-class.bak     base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#            ask defined a dependent as a line that "names the class itself in source" and
#            listed the five ways; baseline wrote one grep per way, 33 of 33, one Read.
#   cycle 5  mastodon.yaml.not-the-call-form.bak   base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#            ask EXCLUDED "dependents that ask the class for something and get an answer
#            back", which narrows gold onto the published-constant and type-test forms -
#            the two rarest, most regex-shaped tokens on this anchor. Baseline 13 of 13,
#            20 Bash calls and exactly ONE Read.
#   cycle 6  (author phase, `Account#local?`)      no run                              n/a
#            NO-ANCHOR reached from a per-row grep census. That is a screen used as a gate,
#            which laws.md forbids in any form; it decided nothing here and is not counted
#            as evidence about the pool.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE.
# From the FAR side. Cycles 4 and 5 sat at baseline 1.000; this draft returns the baseline
# to the ONE measured position inside the window - cycle 2's 0.158 - and moves the sense arm
# from 0.158 up. Intended landing: baseline at or below ~0.20, sense at or above ~0.70.
# It does NOT aim at the opposite of cycle 5; it aims at the point cycle 2 already measured.
#
# THE ONE CONTROLLED CHANGE FROM CYCLE 2. The description and step 2 are cycle 2's, verbatim.
# Only STEP 1 changes, and only to stop it steering the sense arm onto a method surface: it
# now settles on the class as a whole, and closes the cycle-3 storage reading in the same
# sentence. Cycle 2's own report named this exact lever ("if step 1 ended on the record class
# rather than on its finder methods, step 2 would start where the gold lives"), and step 1
# in this form is measured: cycles 4 and 5 used it and the sense arm reached 33 of 33 and
# 13 of 13 respectively, calling sense_blast on the class in both.
# The gold changes only by SUBTRACTION - see the gold header.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KIND OF QUESTION, STATED PLAINLY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ring_sweep.py --top 12` on this clone returns `0 candidate(s) above the bar` - every one
# of the twelve anchors reports 0 rows, 0 files, 0 vias. So "what HOLDS X" is not available
# in this stack and this is KIND ONE, an occurrence-list question, the same kind as this
# repo's banked +0.53 cell and the same kind as the five failures above.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS: it is the ONE line per
# file at which the dependency is actually taken, in files where the name recurs many times
# over for reasons that are not the dependency. The set of files is not the answer and the
# set of occurrences is not the answer; the answer is a selection of one line out of many.
# That said, the honest limit is in `notes`: this is still an occurrence-list question, and
# it can only be the banked sort rather than the failing sort if the ask refuses to name a
# shape. Which sort it is has been measured exactly once, at cycle 2, n=1.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS THE FIRST SORT AND NOT THE SECOND, in one line: it is the only draft of the
# six whose ask names no call form and excludes no dependent kind, and it is the only one
# whose baseline was measured below 0.50.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Account` (app/models/account.rb) is the record every other model, service, worker and
# controller in Mastodon hangs off: local users and remote actors are both rows in it. It is
# the thing a maintainer reworking identity would actually open first. Anchor UNCHANGED for
# the seventh cycle, per laws.md ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, re-run this cycle):
#   Account --file app/models/account.rb
#     app caller files : 107   (total incl test/non-app: 107)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 34
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# Two other candidates profiled this cycle, both worse (precision RANKS, it never kills):
#   Status  --file app/models/status.rb            0.60  (81 callers / 136 grep files, 35 dirs)
#   TagManager --file app/lib/activitypub/tag_manager.rb
#                                                  0.53  (75 callers / 141 grep files, 22 dirs)
#   Instance (no --file needed)                    1.17  - above 1.00 means false caller
#     edges, and 28 caller files cannot carry the group anyway. REJECTED.
# 0.42 is the lowest profiled on this repo across seven cycles.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A dependent of this contract is not about accounts. It consults the record class to settle
# a question about something else - and it does so in whatever form the surrounding code
# already had, so the dependents do not share a shape. What they do share is that the
# record's name recurs throughout their files for reasons that have nothing to do with the
# contract - as documentation, as a column, as an association, as a serializer name, as a
# namespace - while exactly ONE line in the file is the dependency. So finding the file is
# not finding the dependency. The discriminator is which LINE, not which file.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY
# FORM). `Account` appears in 257 app files against 107 real caller files - precision 0.42.
# Nothing in this cycle was decided by a grep; the two-arm run in the next phase decides.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN (bench/lib/mcp_probe.py, this cycle, no min_confidence passed):
#   sense_blast {"symbol":"Account","file":"app/models/account.rb"}
#     -> risk high, risk_factors ["200 direct callers"], 60 direct callers shown over 30
#        areas, truncated true, completeness partial (resolved 60, hidden 276).
# EVERY one of the 14 `dependents` rows below appears in that shown payload, and each row's
# line sits INSIDE the enclosing `def` the payload names - the payload gives the enclosing
# `def`, not the dependency line, so every line below was opened and read by hand this cycle
# (all 23 rows, contract group included; the sed output is what the relations are written
# from, never a script tally).
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, this cycle): the anchor itself comes back at
# closed-book recall 0.258, under the 0.3 bar - "not recited" - and its hits are the class's
# own constants and predicates (USERNAME_RE, MENTION_RE, local, fields...). That is the
# contract's API surface, which is why the contract centre is a NON-SCORING anchor group
# here. Probed per gold row class as well: Form::AccountBatch,
# AccountSuggestions::FriendsOfFriendsSource, ActivityPub::Activity and Admin::BaseController
# all return "only 0 member(s) declared (< 5) - too few to score recitation, bar
# inapplicable". No gold row's credit is a member list; each is a line-level fact at a path
# the probe never produces. Rows CUT for recitation in earlier cycles and still absent:
# FeedManager (0.727) and InstancePresenter (0.842).
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework how a user account record is stored and looked
  up - the record that both local users and remote actors are rows in, and the lookup
  surface the rest of the codebase queries it through. Before you touch it, audit what
  depends on that contract, completely enough that the rework cannot silently break a
  dependent. What counts is the exact line at which a dependency is taken, not the file
  it sits in: for each one give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to,
  plus one phrase on how it uses the contract. A filename with no line number does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, name the
    single record class this ticket is about, say where it is defined, and say what a
    rework of it would have to preserve. Settle on the class as a whole - the audit that
    follows is about everything in the codebase that depends on that class, not about any
    one part of it, not about any one of the things it can do, and not about how it is
    persisted. Give file:line for everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    depends on the account-record contract, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be
    exhaustive and group them by area. Most dependents are not about accounts at all:
    they consult the record class to settle a question about something else entirely,
    and they do it in whatever form the surrounding code already used, so the dependents
    do not share a shape and there is no single thing to look for. In these files the
    record's name also recurs many times over for reasons that have nothing to do with
    the contract, so identifying the file is not the same as identifying the dependency:
    one line in it is the dependency, and that line is what I need. For each dependent
    give file:line and one phrase on how it uses the contract. A filename with no line
    number does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Pinned the dependency line in each file, not just the file
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account record. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (9, NON-SCORING anchors): the record itself, the obvious centre both arms
#     reach, AND the five rows the baseline has taken repeatedly across cycles. It does not
#     discriminate and it is not meant to.
#   - `dependents` (14, THE discriminator): ONE row per FILE, across 9 areas.
#
# THE ONLY GOLD CHANGE FROM CYCLE 2 IS SUBTRACTION, and it is driven by what the arms have
# ACTUALLY cited, not by an estimate (laws.md: A FREE ROW IS A GIFT TO THE BASELINE). Five
# of cycle 2's nineteen `dependents` rows are demoted to the non-scoring `contract` group
# because the baseline has cited them under asks that differ from each other:
#   d:account-owned-concern       taken by the baseline in cycles 2 and 4
#   d:json-ld-helper              taken in cycles 1, 2 and 4
#   d:tag-manager-local-lookup    taken in cycles 2, 4 and 5
#   d:signature-verification      taken in cycles 1, 4 and 5
#   d:action-log-filter           taken in cycles 1 and 5 (same file, cycle 5's line)
# Nothing was added. Adding an unmeasured row dilutes the one measured position this draft
# owns; the fourteen that remain are exactly the rows the baseline missed at 0.158.
#
# The n on that ranking is honest and small: these are single runs per cycle, not the ~5
# per arm `inverse_frequency.py` wants before re-golding. `inverse_frequency.py` was run
# this cycle against the headline model's validation root - it ranks the BANKED Status
# gold (10 runs), not this Account gold, so it could not rank these rows. The per-cycle
# credit tables above are what is on disk for this anchor.
gold:
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: the contract centre ---
  - {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:65 `class Account < ApplicationRecord` - the account record itself, the contract under change"}
  - {id: c:status, group: contract, match: [models/status.rb], relation: "app/models/status.rb:77 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :statuses` - the content model hangs off the account association"}
  - {id: c:user, group: contract, match: [models/user.rb], relation: "app/models/user.rb:72 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :user` - a local login is a User row pointing at an Account row"}
  - {id: c:resolve-service, group: contract, match: [services/resolve_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_account_service.rb:27 `@account ||= Account.find_remote(@username, @domain)` - the webfinger resolve path, the obvious lookup entry point for accounts"}
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: rows the baseline has cited repeatedly across cycles (free) ---
  - {id: c:account-owned-concern, group: contract, match: [account_owned_concern.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/account_owned_concern.rb:21 `@account = username_param.present? ? Account.find_local!(username_param) : Account.local.find(account_id_param)` - the shared account-owned page concern resolves its subject through two class finders"}
  - {id: c:json-ld-helper, group: contract, match: [helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - unsigned fetches fall back to the instance representative account to sign with"}
  - {id: c:tag-manager-local-lookup, group: contract, match: [activitypub/tag_manager.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/tag_manager.rb:260 `Account.local.with_username(usernames).or(Account.local.where(id: ids))` - URI-to-actor translation resolves a batch of local actor URIs through the class scope"}
  - {id: c:signature-verification, group: contract, match: [concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification type-tests the signing actor against the class"}
  - {id: c:action-log-filter, group: contract, match: [admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:114 `account = Account.find_or_initialize_by(id: value)` - filtering the audit log by target builds an unsaved account to match the polymorphic target on"}
  # --- THE DISCRIMINATOR: one dependency line per file, in a file whose subject is elsewhere ---
  # -- inbound federation (app/lib/activitypub)
  - {id: d:activity-uri-resource, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/activity.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity.rb:75 `ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.uri_to_resource(uri, Account)` - inbound activity handling hands the record class to a generic URI resolver as the type to resolve into"}
  - {id: d:activity-create-delivered-to, group: dependents, match: [activity/create.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb:170 `@delivered_to_account ||= Account.find(@options[:delivered_to_account_id])` - status creation memoises the delivery target by id to decide local visibility"}
  # -- federation endpoints (app/controllers/activitypub)
  - {id: d:inbox-unknown-actor, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb:30 `... && !Account.exists?(uri: json['actor'])` - the last clause of a five-clause boolean drops Delete/Update activities for actors the instance has never stored"}
  # -- status-scoped API listings (app/controllers/api/v1/statuses)
  - {id: d:favourited-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 bare `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.without_suspended.includes(:favourites, :account_stat, :user)` - the favourited-by list builds its preload scope off the class"}
  - {id: d:reblogged-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account.without_suspended.includes(:statuses, :account_stat, :user).references(:statuses)` - the reblogged-by list builds its preload scope off the class"}
  # -- remote-follow landing (app/controllers)
  - {id: d:authorize-interactions-dispatch, group: dependents, match: [authorize_interactions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/authorize_interactions_controller.rb:11 `when Account` - the remote-follow landing page branches on the resolved resource being an account rather than a status"}
  # -- admin surfaces (app/controllers/admin, app/controllers/api/v1/admin)
  - {id: d:admin-api-set-account, group: dependents, match: [v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb:103 `@account = Account.find(params[:id])` - the admin API resolves its subject by primary key; the file's other mentions of the name are serializers, services, workers and filters"}
  - {id: d:admin-base-set-user, group: dependents, match: [admin/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/base_controller.rb:21 `@user = Account.find(params[:account_id]).user || raise(ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound)` - the admin base controller reaches a login by going through the account row first"}
  # -- serialization (app/serializers/rest)
  - {id: d:serializer-decorator-model-name, group: dependents, match: [rest/account_serializer.rb], relation: "app/serializers/rest/account_serializer.rb:29 `Account.model_name` - a SimpleDelegator wrapper asks the class for its ActiveModel name so a moved-account delegate serialises as an account"}
  # -- outbound fetch services (app/services/activitypub)
  - {id: d:fetch-remote-actor-typecheck, group: dependents, match: [fetch_remote_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/activitypub/fetch_remote_account_service.rb:7 `return actor if actor.nil? || actor.is_a?(Account)` - remote actor fetching type-tests the fetched actor against the class and rejects anything else"}
  # -- background jobs (app/workers/admin)
  - {id: d:admin-deletion-worker, group: dependents, match: [admin/account_deletion_worker.rb], relation: "app/workers/admin/account_deletion_worker.rb:9 `delete_account = Account.find(account_id)` - the deletion job rehydrates the record from an id carried across the queue boundary"}
  # -- admin form objects (app/models/form)
  - {id: d:account-batch-form, group: dependents, match: [form/account_batch.rb], relation: "app/models/form/account_batch.rb:69 `Account.where(id: account_ids)` - the admin bulk-action form resolves its checked ids back to records through the class"}
  # -- follow suggestions (app/models/account_suggestions)
  - {id: d:suggestions-source, group: dependents, match: [account_suggestions/source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/source.rb:13 bare `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.searchable.where(discoverable: true).without_silenced.without_memorial` - the follow-suggestion base scope is chained off the class"}
  - {id: d:friends-of-friends, group: dependents, match: [friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - the friends-of-friends suggestion runs raw SQL through the account class"}
